 | ID | Date | Decided by | Decision | Rationale / evidence | Reopen if |
 |---|---|---|---|---|---|
-| D-055 | 2026-10-01 | delivery-orchestrator | **DG1 round-9 (F-DG1-129/213/010, all Low/non-mandatory): flip the module lint's Node-built-in and `process.*` checks from DENYLISTS to DEFAULT-DENY ALLOW-LISTS — superseding the "closed as found / exhaustive for the pinned Node" framing of D-053/D-054.** Rounds 4–8 closed loader/exec routes one at a time (`eval`/`constructor`/`Reflect` F-DG1-124; `node:process` F-DG1-125; `node:sqlite` F-DG1-127; `node:test`/`process.execve` F-DG1-128), and round 8 still surfaced `process.kill(pid,'SIGUSR1')` / `process._debugProcess(pid)` (start the in-process V8 inspector — the capability of the banned `node:inspector` — F-DG1-129 code-security and F-DG1-213 qa, the same route found independently), while F-DG1-010 showed the "exhaustive for the pinned Node 22" claim named the wrong runtime (production is Node 24; the sweep ran on Node 22). A denylist over Node's evolving surface cannot converge or be version-independent. **Decision:** `apps/api/src/architecture.testkit.ts` now **allow-lists** the built-ins and process members module source legitimately uses and **denies everything else**: `SAFE_NODE_BUILTINS = {crypto, fs, fs/promises, os, path, url, util}` (bareAllowed: a `node:` specifier is allowed only if in this set) and `SAFE_PROCESS_MEMBERS = {env, exit, argv, once}` (rule 3: `process.<member>` is a violation unless in this set). Every other built-in (module, vm, worker_threads, inspector, repl, child_process, cluster, process, sqlite, test, wasi, v8, net, http, …) and every other process member (kill, _kill, _debugProcess, execve, binding, _linkedBinding, dlopen, getBuiltinModule, abort, …) — **and anything a later Node version adds** — is denied by default. The check is therefore **version-independent** (fixes F-DG1-010's root cause: no per-Node enumeration/sweep) and structurally closes the module-IMPORT and process-MEMBER loader/exec classes version-independently (fixes F-DG1-129/213). It does NOT by itself cover a native-loader MEMBER of an allow-listed module (F-DG1-130, `crypto.setEngine`, name-banned by rule 1) nor the runtime-enumeration reach of such a member (F-DG1-132/215, residual (a)); default-deny is not a claim that no further adjacent route exists. The allow-lists were validated against the real module tree (`moduleViolations` zero): module source imports four of the seven built-ins (`crypto`, `fs`, `path`, `url`) and uses none of the four process members — the remaining entries (`fs/promises`, `os`, `util`; and `env`, `exit`, `argv`, `once`) are **reviewed safe headroom** (read/utility built-ins and read/lifecycle process members a module commonly needs), each confirmed to carry no code-loading, exec, native or debug capability, NOT claimed as current usage (F-DG1-131; a conservative allow-list, kept small). Residuals shrink to the genuinely irreducible: (a) runtime data-flow; (b) runtime code-generation then `import()` of a literal same-module path via an allowed built-in such as `node:fs` (a module may legitimately read files; a write-API-only ban is brittle); (c) `WebAssembly` global instantiation (a wasm exec, not a JS-module loader). The default-deny decides which built-in MODULES a module may import; the MEMBERS of an allow-listed module are covered by a recorded member-audit in the testkit header (F-DG1-130): the only member of the seven that loads/executes/natively-loads code is `crypto.setEngine` (a native `dlopen`), now banned by rule 1 (every spelled form; reaching it by namespace enumeration is residual (a), F-DG1-132/215). The audit was performed on Node v22.22.2 (the build-sandbox floor) and re-run by the orchestrator on Node v24.21.0 (the production target, ADR-0001) in round 11 with the identical result — `crypto.setEngine` the only native-loader member, all dangerous `process.*` members denied by default-deny, and the static lint behaving identically (it parses the AST, independent of the Node runtime); evidence `docs/delivery/test-evidence/DG1/orchestrator/node24-member-audit.log`. It is re-run when the Node floor/target changes. The lint remains static defence-in-depth for the ADR-0002 module boundaries over human-reviewed code in a read-only production source tree — not a runtime security boundary. | DG1 round 8: all three reviewers PASS on candidate `sha256:e27eaf5f…`; the open items (F-DG1-010/129/213) were Low/non-mandatory and all pointed at denylist non-convergence. **Evidence:** `architecture.test.ts` 101/101 incl. default-deny positive/negative controls + the real-module-tree check (`moduleViolations` zero); the full unit suite 294/294 (no legit module import/member newly flagged); an orchestrator probe confirming process.kill/_debugProcess/getBuiltinModule, node:inspector/net/http denied and node:crypto/fs/path/url/util/os + process.env/exit/argv/once allowed; `@mth/api` typecheck/lint/prettier clean; the round-9 freeze + its three PASS reviews + auditor. | A legitimate module needs a built-in or `process` member not on an allow-list (then it is reviewed and added deliberately), or the project adopts an even stricter default-deny for the Node globals (WebAssembly, fetch). |
+| D-055 | 2026-10-01 | delivery-orchestrator | **DG1 round-9 (F-DG1-129/213/010, all Low/non-mandatory): flip the module lint's Node-built-in and `process.*` checks from DENYLISTS to DEFAULT-DENY ALLOW-LISTS — superseding the "closed as found / exhaustive for the pinned Node" framing of D-053/D-054.** Rounds 4–8 closed loader/exec routes one at a time (`eval`/`constructor`/`Reflect` F-DG1-124; `node:process` F-DG1-125; `node:sqlite` F-DG1-127; `node:test`/`process.execve` F-DG1-128), and round 8 still surfaced `process.kill(pid,'SIGUSR1')` / `process._debugProcess(pid)` (start the in-process V8 inspector — the capability of the banned `node:inspector` — F-DG1-129 code-security and F-DG1-213 qa, the same route found independently), while F-DG1-010 showed the "exhaustive for the pinned Node 22" claim named the wrong runtime (production is Node 24; the sweep ran on Node 22). A denylist over Node's evolving surface cannot converge or be version-independent. **Decision:** `apps/api/src/architecture.testkit.ts` now **allow-lists** the built-ins and process members module source legitimately uses and **denies everything else**: `SAFE_NODE_BUILTINS = {crypto, fs, fs/promises, os, path, url, util}` (bareAllowed: a `node:` specifier is allowed only if in this set) and `SAFE_PROCESS_MEMBERS = {env, exit, argv, once}` (rule 3: `process.<member>` is a violation unless in this set). Every other built-in (module, vm, worker_threads, inspector, repl, child_process, cluster, process, sqlite, test, wasi, v8, net, http, …) and every other process member (kill, _kill, _debugProcess, execve, binding, _linkedBinding, dlopen, getBuiltinModule, abort, …) — **and anything a later Node version adds** — is denied by default. The check is therefore **version-independent** (fixes F-DG1-010's root cause: no per-Node enumeration/sweep) and structurally closes the module-IMPORT and process-MEMBER loader/exec classes version-independently (fixes F-DG1-129/213). It does NOT by itself cover a native-loader MEMBER of an allow-listed module (F-DG1-130, `crypto.setEngine`, name-banned by rule 1) nor, by itself, the runtime-enumeration reach of such a member (F-DG1-132/215) — that route is closed by rule 5 (named-imports-only for node:crypto, F-DG1-133); default-deny is not a claim that no further adjacent route exists. The allow-lists were validated against the real module tree (`moduleViolations` zero): module source imports four of the seven built-ins (`crypto`, `fs`, `path`, `url`) and uses none of the four process members — the remaining entries (`fs/promises`, `os`, `util`; and `env`, `exit`, `argv`, `once`) are **reviewed safe headroom** (read/utility built-ins and read/lifecycle process members a module commonly needs), each confirmed to carry no code-loading, exec, native or debug capability, NOT claimed as current usage (F-DG1-131; a conservative allow-list, kept small). Residuals shrink to the genuinely irreducible: (a) runtime data-flow; (b) runtime code-generation then `import()` of a literal same-module path via an allowed built-in such as `node:fs` (a module may legitimately read files; a write-API-only ban is brittle); (c) `WebAssembly` global instantiation (a wasm exec, not a JS-module loader). The default-deny decides which built-in MODULES a module may import; the MEMBERS of an allow-listed module are covered by a recorded member-audit in the testkit header (F-DG1-130): the only member of the seven that loads/executes/natively-loads code is `crypto.setEngine` (a native `dlopen`), now banned by rule 1 (every spelled form) and, since rule 5 (F-DG1-132/133), unreachable by namespace enumeration — a module may import node:crypto through named imports only, so its namespace/default binding never becomes an enumerable value (the former Object.entries(c)/Map route is a violation at the import). The audit was performed on Node v22.22.2 (the build-sandbox floor) and re-run by the orchestrator on Node v24.21.0 (the production target, ADR-0001) in round 11 with the identical result — `crypto.setEngine` the only native-loader member, all dangerous `process.*` members denied by default-deny, and the static lint behaving identically (it parses the AST, independent of the Node runtime); evidence `docs/delivery/test-evidence/DG1/orchestrator/node24-member-audit.log`. It is re-run when the Node floor/target changes. The lint remains static defence-in-depth for the ADR-0002 module boundaries over human-reviewed code in a read-only production source tree — not a runtime security boundary. | DG1 round 8: all three reviewers PASS on candidate `sha256:e27eaf5f…`; the open items (F-DG1-010/129/213) were Low/non-mandatory and all pointed at denylist non-convergence. **Evidence:** `architecture.test.ts` 101/101 incl. default-deny positive/negative controls + the real-module-tree check (`moduleViolations` zero); the full unit suite 294/294 (no legit module import/member newly flagged); an orchestrator probe confirming process.kill/_debugProcess/getBuiltinModule, node:inspector/net/http denied and node:crypto/fs/path/url/util/os + process.env/exit/argv/once allowed; `@mth/api` typecheck/lint/prettier clean; the round-9 freeze + its three PASS reviews + auditor. | A legitimate module needs a built-in or `process` member not on an allow-list (then it is reviewed and added deliberately), or the project adopts an even stricter default-deny for the Node globals (WebAssembly, fetch). |
 | D-054 | 2026-10-01 | delivery-orchestrator | **DG1 round-8 repair (F-DG1-128, Low/non-mandatory): close the last two concrete loader/exec routes and declare the module-lint loader/exec enumeration EXHAUSTIVE for the pinned Node version — superseding D-053's "closed as found" framing.** The round-7 code-security reviewer swept Node 22.22.2's `builtinModules` + `process.*` for loader/exec capabilities not already banned and found exactly two concrete, existing routes that a module could reach and that were not named by any stated residual: **R1** `node:test` `run({ files:[<runtime-computed path>], isolation:"none" })` — an in-process load+run of an on-disk file via a loader built-in (not code generation, so not residual (b)); **R2** global `process.execve` — replaces the process with an arbitrary executable (the class of the banned `child_process`/`cluster`). **Fix (T-DG1-BE9):** add `test` to `LOADER_BUILTINS` (→ `test`/`node:test`; no runtime module uses it, tests use vitest) and `execve` to `PROCESS_LOADERS` (rule 3 flags `process.execve`; the `node:process` import route to it was already closed by F-DG1-125). Self-check R1/R2 added. The testkit header's residual (c) is **tightened**: the concrete loader/exec built-ins and `process.*` methods are now **enumerated exhaustively for the pinned Node 22** (module, vm, worker_threads, inspector, repl, child_process, cluster, process, sqlite, test; process.binding/_linkedBinding/dlopen/execve), as validated by the round-7 sweep; residual (c) is narrowed to genuinely **future/unknown** built-ins of a later Node version and `WebAssembly`/`node:wasi` instantiation (a wasm exec, not a JS-module loader). A later hardening could switch module-source `node:` imports to a default-deny allow-list (module source uses only node:crypto/fs/path/url); deferred (bigger blast radius, not P1). | DG1 round 7: all three reviewers PASS on candidate `sha256:d3743a35…`; the only open item was this Low/non-mandatory finding, raised under D-053's own reopen criterion. CLAUDE.md (a control's stated guarantee must hold). **Evidence:** `architecture.test.ts` 80/80 incl. self-checks R1/R2 + the real module-tree check; `@mth/api` typecheck/eslint/prettier clean; the round-8 freeze + its three PASS reviews + auditor; the round-7 sweep that bounded the enumeration. | A later Node version adds a loader/exec built-in or `process.*` method not in the enumeration (then it is closed like test/execve), or the project adopts the default-deny `node:` allow-list. |
 | D-053 | 2026-10-01 | delivery-orchestrator | **DG1 round-7 repairs (both DG1 round-6 findings Low/non-mandatory): close the concrete native-loader route and formally STATE+ACCEPT the irreducible residual of the module lint, so the denylist does not churn a finding per round.** (1) **F-DG1-127 (code-security): two loader routes outside `LOADER_BUILTINS`.** A1 `node:sqlite` `DatabaseSync(...,{allowExtension:true}).loadExtension(so)` loads a native shared object — the same class as the `process.dlopen` route closed by F-DG1-125. A2 runtime code GENERATION then a dynamic import of a literal same-module path (`writeFileSync(new URL("./gen.mjs",…)); await import("./gen.mjs")`) — the specifier is a legal own-module path and the bytes exist only at runtime, so the lint never sees them. **Fix (T-DG1-BE8):** add `sqlite`/`node:sqlite` to `LOADER_BUILTINS` (a module never needs SQLite — persistence is `@mth/db`/PostgreSQL, ADR-0003; zero imports in src), closing A1 (self-check A1 added). A2 and the general class are **documented and ACCEPTED as stated residuals** in the testkit header, NOT chased: (a) the pre-existing runtime-data-flow residual; (b) runtime code-generation-then-import (node:fs deliberately not banned — tests and future modules legitimately read files; a write-API-only ban is brittle); (c) the loader/eval denylist is ENUMERATED and cannot be proven exhaustive over every host capability (a future Node built-in, `WebAssembly` instantiation) — **known native loaders are closed as found** (`process.dlopen` F-DG1-125, `node:sqlite` F-DG1-127). Rationale: this lint is static defence-in-depth for the ADR-0002 module boundaries, enforced against human-reviewed code that runs with a read-only production source tree — **not** a runtime security boundary; the residuals are accepted, not syntactically closable. (2) **F-DG1-212 (qa): the REQ-DLV-042 cited installer acceptance log was the pre-F-DG1-126 13-case run.** Regenerated `docs/delivery/test-evidence/DG1/orchestrator/install-sandbox-acceptance.log` against the post-fix installer: AC-1..AC-10, 14 passed, 0 failed, exit 0. | DG1 round 6: all three reviewers PASS on candidate `sha256:a7b46fbc…`; the only open items were these two Low/non-mandatory findings. CLAUDE.md (a control's stated guarantee must hold; evidence must match the frozen candidate). **Evidence:** `architecture.test.ts` 78/78 incl. self-check A1 + the real module-tree check; `@mth/api` typecheck/eslint/prettier clean; the regenerated 14/14 installer log; the round-7 freeze + its three PASS reviews + auditor. | A reviewer demonstrates a native/loader route that a module could reach and that is NOT a stated residual (then it is closed like sqlite); or a module legitimately needs `node:fs`/`node:sqlite` (then the ban/residual is revisited). |
 | D-052 | 2026-10-01 | delivery-orchestrator | **DG1 round-6 repairs (both DG1 round-5 findings were Low/non-mandatory; fixed rather than accepted because each is a correctness gap in a security control with a precise, bounded fix).** (1) **F-DG1-125 (code-security): the module-lint native-loader rule 3 was bypassable through an import of `node:process`.** Rule 3 flagged `process.binding/_linkedBinding/dlopen` only when the member's root was the literal global identifier `process`/`globalThis`/`global` (`rootName`); an `import … from "node:process"` (default/namespace/named) aliased the process object to a local binding `rootName` did not recognise, so all three native loaders were reachable from module source with zero violations, contradicting the testkit's "rules 1–2 remove every syntactic route" claim. **Fix (T-DG1-BE7):** add `"process"` and `"node:process"` to `LOADER_BUILTINS` so `bareAllowed()` rejects them and **any** import of the process module in `src/modules/**` is a specifier violation — closing the default, namespace and named import routes at once. `process` is a Node global; a repo-wide grep finds zero imports of it, so there is no legitimate-use cost, and rule 3 still covers the global-identifier form (self-check P12). Self-check extended with X6–X8. (2) **F-DG1-126 (code-security): the installer copy-back's hand-rolled YAML member resolver matched every `- item` line in pnpm-workspace.yaml (not only the `packages:` sequence) and ignored `!` exclusions**, so a negated pattern or an unrelated list-valued key (`publicHoistPattern`, `onlyBuiltDependencies`, …) naming a real directory with a package.json could become a copy-back destination (latent; not reachable on the committed tree, whose workspace file has only `packages:` with `apps/*`, `packages/*`). **Fix:** `tools/deps/install-sandbox.sh` now takes the member list from pnpm's own resolution (`pnpm -r ls --depth -1 --json`, run against the real trusted tree), which honors exactly `packages:` and its `!` exclusions and ignores other keys; the parser merges pnpm's one-array-per-project stream (string/escape-aware), refuses any path outside the repo root, and fails the install on a resolve/parse error (never a silent no-op). AC-10 added. | DG1 round 5: all three reviewers PASS on candidate `sha256:24eb3779…`; the only open items were these two Low/non-mandatory findings. CLAUDE.md ("every mutation/control covered by tests"; a security control's stated guarantee must hold). **Evidence:** installer suite 14/14 (incl. AC-10); the architecture self-check incl. X6–X8 + the real module-tree check green; the round-6 freeze + its three PASS reviews + auditor. | A future pnpm needs a module to import `node:process` (then rule 3 is extended to alias-track the import instead of banning the specifier), or `pnpm -r ls` stops being the authoritative member source. |
